sysc-greet uses greetd's _greeter account. Install a supported compositor,
such as cagebreak, and back up /etc/greetd/config.toml before configuring it.
Install the graphics drivers for your hardware (mesa-dri for Mesa systems).
Install a desktop session too, such as niri or sway; cagebreak does not ship
a session desktop file.

For cagebreak, use:

    [terminal]
    vt = 1

    [default_session]
    command = "cagebreak -e -c /etc/greetd/sysc-greet/cagebreak-greeter-config"
    user = "_greeter"

The package also installs configurations for other compositors under
/etc/greetd/sysc-greet/. Install the compositor you select separately.

Keep an SSH connection or another TTY available while changing login services.
Disable any other display manager and the agetty service on greetd's selected
TTY. Enable the dbus and greetd runit services using the Void handbook:

    https://docs.voidlinux.org/config/services/index.html

D-Bus activates elogind. Do not also enable elogind's runit service.
Reboot after first installing elogind so its udev seat rules take effect.
Use "sv down greetd" over SSH to stop the greeter during recovery, then
"sv up greetd" to restart it after correcting the configuration.

For niri, seat management and turnstile coexistence, see:

    https://nomadcxx.github.io/sysc-greet/docs/getting-started/void-linux/

Use the native paths /usr/bin/sysc-greet, /var/lib/_greeter and
/etc/greetd/sysc-greet/ when adapting instructions from the installer guide.
Run sysc-greet --test inside a terminal to preview without authenticating.

